#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5c788ad2c220b70a7f1d333680a70933f9b490a0db11fb3440dd3a2e06fa01.png)\n\nFind a definite integral that's equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm{d} x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(3,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0+t(3-0),\\,0+t(3-0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t+3t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513" :question/problem "![](images/74/74033bfe17cefe62d5306b2186b9816ba26b816ece10c5dab5178abe1a9aaf89.png)\n\nFind the definite integral equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_C x^2 \\, \\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $25x^2 + 4y^2 = 100,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,5),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 5\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_C \\left(x^2 + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc3a36829c9780c34e13f253d607c3849bfd3ef1399a77a79a3dd3d30ef7140.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40dc00cd-4c24-526f-b0be-714180e4f0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257359} {:answer/id #uuid "5febbd5f-4713-5fff-8f7f-17efac6589cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257622} {:answer/id #uuid "7696a3d2-8e7a-5c22-b23e-1b2752090aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257825} {:answer/id #uuid "ad788cdb-65a4-5dfe-965b-6ccf2a1c8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258298} {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ebaf3f-afef-5801-a181-f3c0188294e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0853e6c9-d856-5aec-b1f2-e5d65484f5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124167" :question/problem "![](images/55/559a6b6cb717c10e60b77da402e310fc5895c814655b4ca1748eba294d712a32.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper half of the circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb77b4b-9f0a-5ae7-b350-e9c8ee7ac7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257019} {:answer/id #uuid "50f9dd65-239e-59ad-8a3d-93b2debc6b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257473} {:answer/id #uuid "6b988f18-d213-513d-9832-3e63e11d42ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257732} {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} {:answer/id #uuid "f63874ba-42bf-5ec0-a115-516773880a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4362a76-2e86-5ca7-8502-9a0cc1964b8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdf631e5-2841-5a67-a8ff-d0eebc8b7e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-124212" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfa95100566fce9ab113b00ae53d1a76e8977b68aa4bac0e9b6892a9930b70d4.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the lower half of the circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66dc688f-81a1-5bab-892b-ab84541785d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387472} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cf7a39-05f3-5b46-9687-4304489da63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387473} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aaa33f-356e-5b31-9322-7fc460783b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387474} {:answer/id #uuid "6904468a-7356-52cb-8fc4-603b5ee7fd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387475} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bac43c-45b2-592b-a306-006e20a13c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cf7a39-05f3-5b46-9687-4304489da63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387473} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a48ec71-cabe-53f3-8125-9985126072d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76ca20f1-204f-59f9-8369-ed13b85fecd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124218" :question/problem "![](images/47/4727c988042bd8d63af370c507db8988b3024cf7e75c86d40bc70cdcfd744873.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01825306-ac24-5300-973f-a0c4b791365b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256796} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27a531-9de1-56f9-836a-e1736077898d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257349} {:answer/id #uuid "8e35d176-d483-5c6c-8219-0e38dad049fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} {:answer/id #uuid "9c536383-f7c3-5cfe-8d3b-1ffc7943f5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258157} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d64c61-750a-55b2-80ed-e2d9402be4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e35d176-d483-5c6c-8219-0e38dad049fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae531638-bc65-5f25-9c4c-6658f8c3500e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591574ce-dde0-5375-8d9f-24f9b5f86769" :question/math-academy-id "q-124223" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3,$ traversed from the point $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $(0,-\\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a914125-7099-5b0c-a1c1-9224eef57080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369260} {:answer/id #uuid "7213e0d0-98dc-5715-bb95-024708c2eadc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369261} {:answer/id #uuid "9a304234-899b-5867-ae14-f933577257a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369262} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0a400c-fbad-505f-8344-98b05be346a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369263} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5940aa-094f-59da-a9fc-f4dda58c537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a304234-899b-5867-ae14-f933577257a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369262} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc543956-16a6-524a-b735-fabf30c10bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55218946-84ed-5872-a1c7-f6fd7a57a20f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124226" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f402fe97e6f76cd94b94331e7f30ddb09c978c93b86ce112a5232042a2f741cc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2168c8cd-5aeb-586f-8d06-cc8367809f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257056} {:answer/id #uuid "2b650bd1-0837-5f08-99d5-a807e4c27e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257170} {:answer/id #uuid "35123647-df55-5d75-89f2-0aca23bfb5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257249} {:answer/id #uuid "59c84fb3-3212-58ee-92d9-6c0807dbf87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257560} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2b2604-95aa-59ca-8d59-13a86f0b5357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59c84fb3-3212-58ee-92d9-6c0807dbf87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257560} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b84b78-85d8-5936-a4d9-bebaf84cdf71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a31726b8-8b33-50ff-9a55-ad044b065273" :question/math-academy-id "q-124234" :question/problem "![](images/43/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(1+t(2-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)+(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3t-1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756639e5-ef49-5356-874a-b797a567ade2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369267} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f350f1-37ad-503f-9ea6-70c30ae57437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369268} {:answer/id #uuid "cb15b44a-28cb-5de2-b373-a77569fe3933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369269} {:answer/id #uuid "be76a975-6edc-58eb-8a4c-da7fbf2781b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369270} {:answer/id #uuid "68681886-c9c0-5332-b1f7-d6c36341760a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3f350f1-37ad-503f-9ea6-70c30ae57437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369268} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea741151-0b0f-527e-9bc4-e1287cde677a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0acc8f31-7302-5dee-af54-ae46e10b8d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124266" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b21c9c11aa3320a729db0d8a71f5c89069d46527e78dfc76b47ae3925387c770.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,2)$ to the point $(-1,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(-1-(-2))),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(t-2)+2(2-t) \\\\  & =3t-6+4-2t \\\\  & =t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce947fb0-2af2-5578-bc88-f97fd8bc5195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369274} {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4e0df-ee12-54ca-90c1-55fd453aaffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369276} {:answer/id #uuid "17c11c14-5bd8-5dfc-9bc8-39959c08d204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369277} {:answer/id #uuid "9230ca12-f5f1-5580-9077-a7bbc7def856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f91420-1dd1-57a3-bbc3-7cd1a732535b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb41f199-2f33-5f68-9a53-dffe3c93ab84" :question/math-academy-id "q-124268" :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57321071-ed92-5969-b645-38b2e65f944b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257537} {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} {:answer/id #uuid "6c038848-0fc0-55d3-b62d-414a628f5e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(e+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257735} {:answer/id #uuid "707e588a-fbf2-5a85-9ad2-4cf1abd166f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257768} {:answer/id #uuid "de648ba3-07ad-5cb0-a4a0-5469d13b5ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae7cf10-da4c-5368-b5cf-081f5c11280f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11aa222d-e9ea-5ecd-ab97-032f5281bcab" :question/math-academy-id "q-124273" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275d71c4-46a9-5261-9eb4-5c0c8e6b9dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257127} {:answer/id #uuid "6e447aa7-08e1-50c3-91b6-e0971243693d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257753} {:answer/id #uuid "88cbd20a-ccc7-55e9-8402-c84e72ce8a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257982} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dc8a41-c7c1-5d26-b4f6-2e6bbd735d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258227} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e80ec8-d12c-5842-b5d1-daee11dbe703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e447aa7-08e1-50c3-91b6-e0971243693d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257753} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d13a439-5bf8-5f5d-9ec6-8ae8c6bdbf6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eef0fe8-9d36-5fca-a5de-d79c02a74295" :question/math-academy-id "q-124279" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d55ece1948f9ecb7da7d489b86a297651a34f409f83ba2470f6dd06678a375e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f83140-8d60-5e3b-8557-38a73fea7b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256790} {:answer/id #uuid "130cd1bb-c702-58af-b317-816f00770ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256955} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8051c9-5f19-5e7c-9a70-1cd1c77f1c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257745} {:answer/id #uuid "933f36c7-2d77-5384-a819-869eaeb53ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258081} {:answer/id #uuid "b62bc8f9-e700-500d-bcd6-4f8a56698270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d8051c9-5f19-5e7c-9a70-1cd1c77f1c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257745} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e765f7a-7a63-5189-8586-c70b56370d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76e58dcc-8bc1-5f3d-ae09-2c38a29543eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124287" :question/problem "![](images/69/69a10b5524670d31cfc9f2982e37d58a5ee9ca39fe49f35b9dace011275a635a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(9-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along three-quarters of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(1,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc88e3d-a852-5587-8a01-ed034f9f4385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387493} {:answer/id #uuid "0354e92a-880d-5b37-9463-ad3a8ea451ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387494} {:answer/id #uuid "05b857f2-d2e7-58cf-af80-9a20296d1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387495} {:answer/id #uuid "5303fcf8-09a5-5b94-af0f-54b031ab7e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387496} {:answer/id #uuid "29b535f4-dc26-5f10-9f77-d35c710a0661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b857f2-d2e7-58cf-af80-9a20296d1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387495} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af7ee7b-5d10-5025-8624-92a4d018ecb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e84b6989-0b06-5564-bfd3-0660ddb2a037" :question/math-academy-id "q-124293" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d02a56e1f927ccf8bd76a6fcc46dec61259649176695912865dd050657ce7f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
